// XFAIL: DF-226a a closure body's TAIL never receives the expected RETURN type,
// so a bare literal there does not adopt it — a fixed-width return ICEs
//
// Found while writing design 226 unit 3's C-callback comparator, and PRE-DATING
// it: nothing about a `FuncPointer` is involved. `_check_closure` pushes the
// expected return type onto the body for exactly two shapes — an OPTIONAL (so a
// bare `None` learns what it is a `None` of, DF-146c) and a NEVER tail — and
// never calls `_apply_literal_expected_type` with it. That funnel is what gives
// every other typed slot in the language its literal treatment, so a closure
// tail is missing all of it at once.
//
// The mechanism reaches more than one position (obligation 4, probed):
//   * FIXED-WIDTH ADOPTION, below — the tail stays platform `Int` and the
//     closure is typed `(Int32) -> Int` against an expected `(Int32) -> Int32`.
//     Codegen builds the body at the WRONG width and dies in the LLVM parser
//     (`ret i64` from an `i32` function) or, one layer out, as a closure-triple
//     type mismatch. An internal compiler error either way.
//   * COLLECTION SHAPING — `runv({ x in [1, 2, 3] })` against
//     `(Int) sync -> Vector<Int>` is a clean but wrong error, ``got `(Int) ->
//     [Int; 3]` ``: with no expectation the array literal never becomes a
//     Vector. Same missing call, milder symptom.
// A suffixed literal works today (`1i32`), which is the shape design 226's
// FFI test uses in the meantime.
//
// The fix is one call at the point the return context is already computed, but
// it is a position-quantified rule and owes the sweep obligation 4 asks for:
// the tail is one of several places a closure body meets an expected type
// (arm results inside the tail, a `return` statement in the body, the
// auto-wrap paths beside it).
//
// EXPECT: success
// EXPECT-OUTPUT:
// bare = 1
// arms = 7

func run(f: (Int32) sync -> Int32) -> Int32 { f(3) }

func main() {
    let bare = run({ x in 1 })
    print("bare = {bare}")
    let arms = run({ x in if x > 0 { 7 } else { 0 } })
    print("arms = {arms}")
}
